{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`_inherited_child_impact` (src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py:241, ticket 3e4b) lets a\ncontainer inherit its direct children's declared `file_impact` as its drift scope, under a\nPOISON RULE: any non-closed child with an empty `file_impact` collapses the union to `{}`, and\nthe container falls back to whole-HEAD invalidation. The rule is right for silence and wrong\nfor an explicit claim — one genuinely-no-file-impact child (a coordination child, an\noperator-action child)\ncurrently discards every sibling's careful declaration and makes the parent re-review on any\ncommit in the repo. `p9_file_impact_coverage` (src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_lint.py:267)\nnames that child as a poisoner for the same reason.\n\nWith the tri-state from S1 [rebar:1934-63dd-4be9-4c48] and the scope kinds from S2 [rebar:749c-7546-6fe3-4d27], both\ncan distinguish the two cases.\n\n## Approach\n\nChange the poison test from \"empty `file_impact`\" to \"UNDECLARED file-impact scope\". A non-closed\ndirect child is then one of three things:\n\n- `paths` → contributes its paths (unchanged);\n- `none` → NEUTRAL: contributes nothing and does not poison — its truthful claim is that no\n  file of the repo is in its scope, which adds nothing to the union and removes nothing from\n  its precision;\n- `undeclared` → poisons, as today (a partial union is fail-open for unknown scope).\n\n`_inherited_child_impact` returns the union as now, but the caller needs to distinguish \"empty\nbecause every live child declared none\" from \"empty because there are no children\" — the first\nis scope `none`, the second leaves the container on its own declaration. The function\ntherefore returns a small result carrying both the path set and whether inheritance was\nEFFECTIVE and all-none, and `dependency_hashes` / the S2 scope classifier consume that:\n\n- container with any `paths` child → deps = union (scope `paths`);\n- container whose non-closed children ALL declare `none`, and whose own impact is `none` or\n  empty → scope `none`;\n- container with an undeclared live child → deps as today (scope `unscoped` unless the\n  container's own file_impact or citations scope it).\n\nClosed children keep today's semantics: they neither contribute nor poison.\n\n`p9_file_impact_coverage` follows the same rule: a LEAF with an explicit `none` PASSES with\ncoverage recording the declared kind; a CONTAINER's poisoning list counts only undeclared\nchildren. The advisory text for a still-undeclared leaf gains the `--none` remedy alongside\n`set_file_impact`, so the coaching names both exits.\n\n**Integration ownership.** S3 owns the container handshake: it passes `container_all_none=_inherited_child_impact(...).all_none` into `classify_file_scope` introduced by S2 at the `dependency_hashes`/manifest call site. S2 owns explicit leaf `none`; S3 owns promotion of an all-none live-child container to effective scope `none`. S3 runs after S4 so its `orchestrator.py` edit incorporates the S4 `applies(..., file_impact_scope=...)` signature rather than racing the shared file.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py` — `_inherited_child_impact` tri-state result and its\n  use in `dependency_hashes` / the scope classifier added in S2.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_lint.py` — `p9_file_impact_coverage` leaf + container rules\n  and advisory text.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py` — confirm the child states fetched for P5/P8\n  (line ~207) carry `file_impact_scope`; extend the projection if the fetch is field-limited.\n\nOut of scope: the write surface (S1), the manifest/validity plumbing itself (S2), the review\ncriterion (S4).\n\n## Testing\n\n- `_inherited_child_impact` unit table: children ∈ {paths, none, undeclared, closed-anything}\n  in the combinations that matter — paths+none → the paths union; all-none → empty-and-all-none;\n  paths+undeclared → poisoned; none+closed-undeclared → still all-none (a closed child never\n  poisons).\n- End-to-end scope test: a container with one paths child and one none child signs an\n  attestation scoped to the paths child's files, and a commit touching an UNRELATED file does\n  not invalidate it, while a commit touching the declared file does.\n- P9 table: leaf/none → pass; leaf/undeclared → advisory (unchanged); container with a none\n  child and no undeclared child → pass; container with an undeclared child → advisory naming\n  only that child.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A container with children `{A: paths, B: none}` inherits exactly A's paths; the signed\n      dependency map contains A's files and nothing else (checked: unit test over\n      `_inherited_child_impact` plus a `dependency_hashes` assertion).\n- [ ] That container's attestation survives a commit to a file declared by NO child, and is\n      invalidated by a commit to A's declared file (checked: `compute_validity` test).\n- [ ] A container whose non-closed children ALL declare `none` and whose own impact is `none` or empty is classified scope `none` and\n      survives an unrelated commit (checked: scope-classifier + validity test).\n- [ ] A container with one UNDECLARED non-closed child is still poisoned to the whole-HEAD\n      fallback (checked: unchanged expectation in the same table).\n- [ ] P9 passes for a leaf declaring `none` and still advises a leaf that declared nothing,\n      with the advisory naming the `--none` remedy (checked: det_lint test asserting the\n      result status and the remedy string).\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the full test suite pass.\n\n## Review remediation: concrete integration handshake\n\nThis section supersedes any earlier ambiguity about result shape or call-site ownership. Add a frozen private `_InheritedChildImpact` dataclass in `manifest.py` with `paths: frozenset[str]`, `effective: bool`, and `all_none: bool`. `effective` is true only when at least one non-closed child exists and every such child has an authenticated `paths` or `none` scope; `all_none` is true only when effective and every non-closed child scope is `none`. Closed children are ignored. Any live `undeclared` child returns an ineffective, empty result.\n\n`dependency_hashes` gains an optional private `child_impact` keyword. When absent it computes `_inherited_child_impact(children)` for existing callers; when supplied it uses that exact object and adds only `child_impact.paths`. In `attest.py`, `sign_plan_review` computes `child_impact = _inherited_child_impact(children)` once, passes it to `dependency_hashes`, and calls `classify_file_scope(..., container_all_none=child_impact.all_none)`. This is the only signing-boundary handshake; `dependency_hashes` keeps its public `dict[str, str]` return type.\n\nAdd `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py` to scope for that wiring. No `orchestrator.py` edit is required: the existing `_reads.show_ticket` call fetches full child state, including `file_impact_scope`; a behavioral test through assembled `PlanContext.children` proves the field reaches P9. The existing private helper has only the `dependency_hashes` caller; the new optional precomputed argument provides expand-contract compatibility while `attest.py` migrates.\n\nAC3 is specifically guarded: all live children must declare `none` AND the container own impact must be `none` or empty. A container with own `paths` remains `paths`; it is never promoted to `none`. Mutation proof uses the focused applicability node already recorded by the prior TDD session: temporarily restore the old empty-impact poison predicate, run the focused child-scope table to observe RED, restore the tri-state predicate, and rerun GREEN."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785244801626359001,"uuid":"3cfb4786-8c73-4a50-a5e7-4db8b1923722"}